INT. GREENHOUSE AT A DEFUNCTIONING ORCHID BREEDER'S - NIGHT

Steam clings to the glass like breath on a window. Rows of orchids glow under purple grow lamps, their petals speckled like skin, and the air is warm enough to loosen a collar. A long potting table has been set for a dinner nobody quite wanted: four chipped plates, a bottle of wine, a bowl of lemons.

ISOLDE FAIRWEATHER, thirty-five, the breeder, in a canvas apron over a silk dress, pours wine with the steadiness of someone holding her hand still on purpose. Across from her, THEODORE "TEDDY" NAKAMURA, thirty-eight, who once loved her and has returned to buy the business, loosens his tie and looks everywhere but at her. At the end of the table sits PERPETUA OYELARAN, seventy, Isolde's aunt, picking a lemon apart with her fingernails. Beside her, his phone face-down by his plate, is ANTON LUNDGREN, forty, Isolde's fiancé, who has just realized who Teddy is.

ISOLDE
You flew eleven hours to look at flowers.

TEDDY
I flew eleven hours for the Phalaenopsis line. The flowers are incidental.

ANTON
She doesn't sell the line.

PERPETUA
Everything's for sale at the right hour of the night, Anton.

ISOLDE
Aunt Perpetua.

TEDDY
She isn't wrong.

ANTON
You two have history. Say it. I'd rather hear it than guess it.

ISOLDE
Anton, not here.

PERPETUA
I'd say here's the best place. Nothing here can hear but the orchids.

TEDDY
We were engaged. For nine months. It ended before you met her.

ANTON
She told me it was a boy she knew at school.

ISOLDE
It was easier.

PERPETUA
It always is.

TEDDY
Isolde, I'm not here to cause --

ANTON
Then why are you here, Teddy? Honestly. Why is there a man at my table who knows which side of the bed she sleeps on?

ISOLDE
That's enough!

PERPETUA
Left. She sleeps on the left.

ANTON
Perpetua!

Isolde stands. The bottle tips and wine runs across the table toward the lemons. Teddy reaches to catch it at the same moment Anton shoves his chair back, and the two men collide at the corner of the table, the chair crashing into a rack of seedlings, Perpetua calmly lifting her plate out of the flood and Isolde pressing both palms to her face.

Then, in the quiet of the falling trays, a single orchid stem snaps.

ISOLDE
(barely audible)
That one was nine years old.
